[
  {
    "claim": "The paper argues that previous research has largely overlooked the interaction between personal political orientation and local political climate, focusing instead on their unique impacts separately.",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "text": "While previous research has identified the unique impact of both political orientation and political climate on vaccination attitudes, there has been little research on the interaction between personal political orientation and local political climate.",
        "page": 2
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "The paper suggests that the lack of a significant interaction effect for vaccine status, compared to hesitancy and booster status, may be due to the prevalence of COVID-19 vaccine mandates influencing initial uptake.",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "text": "The interaction for vaccination was not significant, this difference between attitudes (hesitancy) and action (vaccination), likely reflects the high prevalence of COVID-19 vaccine mandates.",
        "page": 10
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
  {
    "claim": "Participants were recruited via Qualtrics Panels using quota sampling for race (Black and White) and income (above and below the US median of $70,000).",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "text": "the sample was recruited across four quota groups based on race and income. Half of the sample was requested to be White... and half the sample was requested to be Black... Within each racial group, half of the participants were requested to be below the median annual income in the US [$70,000]",
        "page": 3
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "Regional political climate was measured by calculating the percentage of Republican votes in the 2020 presidential election for each participant's zip code.",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "text": "Using the FIPS data for each zip code, we calculated the percent of Republican votes by dividing the number of Republican votes by the total number of votes (multiplied by 100). We used this % Republican vote by zip code as a measure of conservative regional climate.",
        "page": 3
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "Vaccine hesitancy was assessed using a modified seven-item Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale, and booster hesitancy was assessed using items from a 2022 survey.",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "text": "Vaccine hesitancy... was measured using the seven items from the Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale... We modified the items to assess attitudes specifically toward the COVID-19 vaccine... Booster hesitancy was assessed using items from a 2022 survey on COVID-19 booster hesitancy",
        "page": 3
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "The final analytical sample consisted of 683 participants after excluding those who failed quality screens, provided illogical open-ended responses, or lacked valid zip code data.",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "text": "We eliminated an additional nine participants whose open-ended responses did not make logical sense... We eliminated an additional 115 participants who did not provide valid zip code data... The final sample for these analyses included 683 participants.",
        "page": 5
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
  {
    "claim": "The paper builds on the view that political conservatism is historically associated with lower trust in the scientific community, which subsequently links to vaccine hesitancy.",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "text": "While political conservatism has been historically associated with lower trust in the scientific community, which is subsequently linked to vaccine hesitancy [15,16], the increased partisan divide in vaccine hesitancy may be attributed, at least in part, to the politicization of COVID-19 [17].",
        "page": 2
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "The paper acknowledges prior research identifying the unique impact of political orientation and political climate on vaccination attitudes but notes the lack of research on their interaction.",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "text": "While previous research has identified the unique impact of both political orientation and political climate on vaccination attitudes, there has been little research on the interaction between personal political orientation and local political climate.",
        "page": 2
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "The paper cites evidence that living in states with higher Republican vote shares is associated with higher likelihood of reporting vaccine hesitancy.",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "text": "Similarly, those who lived in states with higher Republican vote shares in the 2020 presidential election were more likely to report vaccine hesitancy in a 2021 sample of 443,680 participants [21].",
        "page": 2
      }
    ]
  }
]

{
"scope_and_limits": [
{
"claim": "The study acknowledges that using zip codes as a proxy for political climate may not account for other regional factors like population density, and that the reliance on self-reported data introduces potential biases.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that other zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper.",
"page": 10
},
{
"text": "Another limitation in this dataset is the reliance on self-reported vaccine status. Self-report is susceptible to social desirability [30] and recall bias [31], and we cannot assume that reported vaccine status reflects actual vaccine status.",
"page": 10
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The authors state that the cross-sectional nature of the data prevents them from assuming causal or directional associations.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Finally, it should be noted that this data is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.",
"page": 10
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The sample is limited to White and Black participants recruited through an online platform, which may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through Qualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.",
"page": 10
}
]
}
],
"evidence": [
{
"claim": "The study used an online survey of 683 participants collected between March 8 and April 19, 2023, recruiting a convenience sample through Qualtrics Panels with quotas for race and income.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "We collected online survey data from 683 participants between March 8 and April 19, 2023.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "This was an online self-reported study in which a nonprobability convenience sample was recruited from the United States between March 8 and April 19, 2023.",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "Participants were a convenience sample recruited through Qualtrics Panels, an online research platform that manages participant recruitment and data quality for academic studies.",
"page": 3
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Regional political climate was measured by calculating the percentage of Republican votes in the 2020 presidential election based on participants' zip codes.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "We assessed regional political climate using zip code data provided by participants and 2020 presidential election results sourced from the New York Times [22].",
"page": 3
},
{
"text": "Using the FIPS data for each zip code, we calculated the percent of Republican votes by dividing the number of Republican votes by the total number of votes (multiplied by 100).",
"page": 3
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Political conservatism was assessed by averaging responses to two items measuring social and economic conservatism on an 11-point sliding scale from strongly liberal (0) to strongly conservative (10).",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Political conservatism ... was assessed across two items. The first item, assessing social conservatism ... asked participants to indicate their political orientation on “social issues” ... The second item, assessing economic conservatism ... asked participants to indicate their political orientation on “economic issues”.",
"page": 3
},
{
"text": "Both items were rated on a 11-point sliding scale from strongly liberal (0) to strongly conservative (10). These two items were averaged to form a political conservatism scale.",
"page": 3
}
]
}
]
}
